  "summary": "Sequencing-time and bisulfite-aware k-mer methods are emerging as tools for inference from partial or converted sequence data.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints, k-mer representations underpin sequence inference without full alignment. ACOR builds a streaming k-mer ledger and applies length-constrained beam search, enabling DNA-storage reconstruction during ongoing sequencing. biHash is a rolling hash for bisulfite-sequenced DNA that maps bisulfite-compatible k-mer variants to a shared hash, with a reported speedup of about 13-fold. The canine study applies alignment-free k-mer analysis to buccal and cell-free DNA, finding age-associated signals stronger in buccal DNA and using ridge models to predict age. What is new is the explicit imposition of domain constraints, such as length bounds or bisulfite equivalence, on standard k-mer machinery. Performance claims should be read against each paper's own benchmarks, since the application domains differ.",
